=0A=0AHi ${first_name},=0A=0AThe risk profile of business AI changes when a=
n agent stops answering questions=0Aand starts crossing system boundaries. =
A model connected to ERP, CRM, email,=0Afile storage, and workflow tools do=
es not just produce an answer. It can=0Aassemble records that were previous=
ly separated, act through credentials that=0Aoutlive the task, and carry on=
e compromised integration into several operating=0Asystems.=0A=0AThis issue=
 is about the layer replacing traditional prompt review: system access=0Ama=
pping. The useful question is no longer only whether the output is correct.=
 It=0Ais what the integration can reach, combine, and change.=0A=0ABill=0A=
=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE SHIFT=0A=0A=0ASystem Access Is Becoming the Hidden AI Ri=
sk.=0A=0AThe model is not the blast radius. The permission map is.=0A=0AThe=
 first wave of business AI review centered on the model. Did it make up a=
=0Afact. Did a person check the response. Could sensitive data appear in th=
e prompt=0Aor answer. Those controls still matter, but they describe an ass=
istant sitting=0Aon one side of a screen. Agents change the unit of risk.=
=0A=0AFive signals from 2025 and 2026=0A=0A=C2=B7 16% of respondents were a=
lready using agents for multi-step workflows and=0Amulti-agent systems, whi=
le only 26% said leadership was clearly aligned on AI.=0AAdoption is outpac=
ing control (Microsoft 2026 Work Trend Index=0A[https://www.microsoft.com/e=
n-us/worklab/work-trend-index/agents-human-agency-and-the-opportunity-for-e=
very-organization]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 45 non-human identities exist for every hu=
man identity. These include service=0Aaccounts, API keys, and secrets used =
by applications and automated systems.=0AAgents use those identities to acc=
ess sensitive systems and data (Cloud Security=0AAlliance=0A[https://clouds=
ecurityalliance.org/artifacts/securing-non-human-identities-in-the-age-of-a=
i-agents-rsac-2025]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 Tool Misuse and Identity Abuse are now di=
stinct risks in OWASP's Top 10 for=0AAgentic Applications. An agent does no=
t need an exotic exploit if it has=0Alegitimate access to export customer r=
ecords, modify an order, send an email, or=0Atrigger a workflow (OWASP Top =
10 for Agentic Applications=0A[https://genai.owasp.org/2025/12/09/owasp-gen=
ai-security-project-releases-top-10-risks-and-mitigations-for-agentic-ai-se=
curity/]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 One compromised OAuth token associated with Saleslof=
t Drift was used to export=0Alarge volumes of Salesforce data. The same tok=
ens provided access to connected=0AGoogle Workspace accounts. One integrati=
on became the attack path across=0Amultiple systems (Google Threat Intellig=
ence Group=0A[https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/data=
-theft-salesforce-instances-via-salesloft-drift]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 13% of breac=
hed organizations reported breaches of AI models or applications,=0Aand 97%=
 of that group lacked proper AI access controls. Sixty percent of=0AAI-rela=
ted incidents led to compromised data and 31% caused operational=0Adisrupti=
on (IBM 2025 Cost of a Data Breach=0A[https://newsroom.ibm.com/2025-07-30-i=
bm-report-13-of-organizations-reported-breaches-of-ai-models-or-application=
s%2C-97-of-which-reported-lacking-proper-ai-access-controls]).=0A=0AMicroso=
ft's data shows the move into multi-step workflows is already underway,=0Aw=
hile leadership alignment trails behind. That work needs credentials. CSA's=
=0Aidentity count shows how quickly those credentials disappear into a much=
 larger=0Apopulation of service accounts, tokens, keys, and application ide=
ntities. OWASP=0Athen identifies the failure that becomes possible: a tool =
can be misused while=0Athe agent remains inside the access it was granted.=
=0A=0AThe Salesloft Drift incident makes the operating consequence concrete=
. One=0Aconnected application held OAuth paths into Salesforce and email. =
When the=0Atokens were compromised, the response could not stop at reviewin=
g the app.=0ACustomers had to trace every connected system, rotate credenti=
als, and search=0Athe exported data for more secrets that could extend the =
breach again. IBM's=0Afindings show what happens when that control gap reac=
hes AI systems: data loss=0Aand operational disruption, not just a bad resp=
onse in a chat window.=0A=0AFor a manufacturer or distributor, the blast ra=
dius follows the workflow. An=0Aorder-status agent may need ERP reads, cust=
omer emails, shipping documents, and=0Aworkflow actions. A purchasing agent=
 may touch supplier records, inventory,=0Apurchase approvals, and shared fi=
les. If each connection inherits a broad=0Aadministrator or employee creden=
tial, the agent quietly becomes a bridge across=0Acontrols that used to be =
separate.=0A=0AModel review asks what the AI said. Access review asks what =
the entire connected=0Aworkflow can do if the agent, token, source document=
, or integration is wrong.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AFROM THE FLOOR=0A=0A=0ACompanie=
s are already exposed without realizing it.=0A=0AKeren Katz, OWASP, on the =
visibility gap around deployed agents=0A=0A=E2=80=9CCompanies are already e=
xposed to Agentic AI attacks. Often without realizing=0Athat agents are run=
ning in their environments.=E2=80=9D=0A=0AKeren Katz=0A=0ASenior Group Mana=
ger of AI Security, Tenable =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 Co-Lead, OWASP Top 10 for=0A=
Agentic Applications=0A[https://genai.owasp.org/2025/12/09/owasp-genai-secu=
rity-project-releases-top-10-risks-and-mitigations-for-agentic-ai-security/=
]=0A=0AKatz co-led the OWASP Top 10 effort that gathered input from more th=
an 100=0Asecurity researchers, practitioners, user organizations, and techn=
ology=0Aproviders. Her statement reflects what most organizations are disco=
vering right=0Anow: agents are already connected to business systems, movin=
g data, and taking=0Aactions. The visibility gap is structural. Most agent =
reviews document the=0Aprompt, model, owner, and intended task. That leaves=
 the operational exposure=0Ascattered across ERP roles, OAuth grants, share=
d accounts, API keys, email=0Apermissions, folder access, and workflow acti=
ons.=0A=0AThe takeaway=0A=0ANo single screen shows that an order-status age=
nt can read margin data, retrieve=0Afiles, send customer email, and update =
a case because each permission was=0Aapproved in a different system. The op=
erating risk is not visible until the=0Apermissions are mapped together.=0A=
=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE STACK=0A=0A=0AThe Agent Blast-Radius Map.=0A=0AOne opera=
ting view before a useful integration becomes a shared point of=0Afailure.=
=0A=0AMap the full reach before one agent becomes the shortest path through=
 the=0Abusiness.=0A=0AMost agent reviews document the prompt, model, owner,=
 and intended task. That=0Aleaves the operational exposure scattered across=
 ERP roles, OAuth grants, shared=0Aaccounts, API keys, email permissions, f=
older access, and workflow actions. No=0Asingle screen shows that an order-=
status agent can read margin data, retrieve=0Afiles, send customer email, a=
nd update a case because each permission was=0Aapproved in a different syst=
em. The Agent Blast-Radius Map pulls those=0Aconnections into one operating=
 view before a useful integration becomes a shared=0Apoint of failure.=0A=
=0AWhat the map shows=0A=0A=C2=B7 The business job. Agent name, owner, inte=
nded task, and named business=0Acontext.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Connected systems and =
actions. ERP, CRM, email, file repositories, workflow=0Atools. For each, wh=
ether the agent can read, create, update, delete, approve,=0Asend, export, =
or trigger.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Credentials and ownership. Service account, API key=
, OAuth grant, shared=0Aidentity. Who issued it, when it was created, when =
it expires, who owns it now.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Sensitive data reached. Customer r=
ecords, order data, pricing, supplier=0Ainformation, financial commitments.=
 Data classification and approval thresholds.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Gaps and risks. P=
ermissions beyond the named job, credentials with no=0Aexpiration, shared i=
dentities, unnecessary system connections, write access=0Awithout approval =
gates.=0A=0AHow it works in practice=0A=0AThe mapping process=0A=0AStep 1. =
Name the agent and the job. What is it called, who owns it, and what=0Abusi=
ness work does it do. One agent, one job. If the agent touches multiple=0Aw=
orkflows, map them separately or consolidate the job definition first.=0A=
=0AStep 2. List every system it touches. ERP, CRM, email, file storage, wor=
kflow=0Aautomation, external APIs. For each system, record what permissions=
 the agent=0Aholds: read, create, update, delete, approve, send, export, tr=
igger.=0A=0AStep 3. Trace the credentials. What identity does each connecti=
on use. Service=0Aaccount, API key, OAuth grant, shared account. Who owns i=
t now, when was it=0Acreated, when does it expire, what else can it reach i=
n each system.=0A=0AStep 4. Identify gaps. Compare actual access against th=
e minimum needed to do=0Athe named job. Anything outside that path gets fla=
gged: excessive read scope,=0Awrite access without approval, unnecessary sy=
stem, shared identity, credential=0Awith no expiration, inherited permissio=
ns from a previous workflow.=0A=0ABuilding it: Two paths=0A=0APath 1: Inter=
nal build=0A=0AStart with the three agents or copilots closest to customer =
orders, purchasing,=0Ainventory, finance, or production scheduling. Export =
enterprise-application=0Agrants from the identity provider, connected-app p=
ermissions from CRM,=0Aintegration users and roles from ERP, mailbox delega=
tion from the email=0Aplatform, and service credentials from the secrets ma=
nager. Join them in a=0Acontrolled table using the agent or service identit=
y as the key. Add a fixed=0Aaction vocabulary: read, create, update, delete=
, approve, send, export, trigger.=0AHave the business owner confirm the min=
imum actions needed for the named job,=0Athen flag every mismatch. A spread=
sheet, diagram, and monthly credential review=0Aare enough for the first ve=
rsion if one owner is accountable for keeping them=0Acurrent.=0A=0APath 2: =
ERP or partner build=0A=0AAsk the ERP partner, systems integrator, or manag=
ed service provider to map the=0Aagent's complete transaction path rather t=
han reviewing only the ERP role. The=0Apartner can pull integration users, =
role permissions, API scopes, workflow=0Ascripts, connected applications, a=
nd audit history from the systems it already=0Amanages, then join those rec=
ords with identity-provider and email permissions.=0AThe useful deliverable=
 is not another security report. It is a live operating=0Amap tied to order=
 entry, purchasing, inventory, production, and customer=0Acommunication, wi=
th named shutoff steps and a permission-change queue the=0Ainternal team ca=
n review.=0A=0ATreat every agent as its own worker identity. Give it only t=
he systems and=0Aactions required for one named job. Make the combined reac=
h visible before the=0Aworkflow goes live.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE OPERATIONS =
BRIEF=0A=0ABy Bill Murphy =C2=A0=C2=B7=C2=A0 Powered by Colony Spark [https=
://www.colonyspark.com]=0A=0AAlready mapping your agent access, or discover=
ing agents connected to critical=0Asystems you did not know about. Hit repl=
y, I read every one. Bill=0A=0AYou are receiving this because you subscribe=
d to The Operations Brief.=0AUnsubscribe [[[custom:unsubscribe]]]
